//! #7058: yq compares two `!!timestamp` values as times in `sort`, `sort_by`, `min` and `max`, not as
//! text: a `with_dtf` layout (`02-Jan-2006`) and RFC 3339 with mixed offsets or fractional seconds
//! sort by instant. In `min`/`max` a timestamp element reads the pick as a time too, so a pick that
//! is no timestamp raises the error reading it as one gives, and a timestamp pick against a string
//! that is none is refused. Two other strings stay bytewise. Every row is `yq -o=json -I=0 FILTER`
//! on the document, captured from yq v4.53.3. A quoted date is read as a timestamp as well, as in
//! yq, which compares what the text parses as.

use anyhow::Result;

